import Native

{-
   The SHA-1 and SHA-256 compressions, on a target that has no instructions for them.

   Two one-line functions, and the file they are in is the whole mechanism: `nosha` is the half of
   the selector that answers for every target `Hardware.sha.yana` does not - a JavaScript build, a
   machine that is not x86-64, and an x86-64 build that did not claim the extension. Exactly one of
   the two files is read, so exactly one definition of each of these names exists.

   **Why a file and not an `if`.** A compile-time predicate would leave the untaken arm resolved,
   and resolved IR reaches a backend in any build with the optimizer off - which is every fixture in
   this tree, compiled a second time that way on purpose. The backend would then be handed
   instructions its target cannot encode. What the selector drops was never resolved.

   See `Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana`, where the same argument is made from the other side, and `targetSelector`,
   where the pair of names is declared.
-}

pub fn sha256CompressBlocks(&h: [U32 *8], from: [U8], at: Size, blocks: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < blocks:
        sha256Compress(h, from, at + i * 64)
        i = i + 1

pub fn sha1CompressBlocks(&h: [U32 *5], from: [U8], at: Size, blocks: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < blocks:
        sha1Compress(h, from, at + i * 64)
        i = i + 1
